                            Preamble

The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other works, specifically designed to ensure cooperation
with the community in the case of network server use.  The license is
affero (AGPL) copyleft version of the GPL.  The AGPL was written for
authors who want to make sure that when users run modified versions of
the software on a server, those users also get the source code.

When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
them if you wish), that you receive source code or can get it if you
want it, that you can change the software or use pieces of it in new
free programs, and that you know you can do these things.

Developers that use our General Public Licenses protect your rights
with two steps: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer
you this License which gives you legal permission to copy, distribute
and/or modify the software.

A license is "copyleft" when the derivative works of that license must
also be available under the same license.  The GNU General Public License
is copyleft; the GNU Lesser General Public License (LGPL) is less
copyleft.  What's more, the AGPL ensures that the source code for a
modified version of the software is made available to the users of that
version, not just to the people who received the software directly from
you.

The precise terms and conditions for copying, distribution and
modification follow.

